# Findings the image scans (.github/workflows/security-scan.yml, and the
# pre-push scan in publish.yml) may skip. Each entry says why, and each reason
# is one that a pin cannot change. Remove an entry the day it stops being true.
#
# Every entry carries three dates, on comment lines directly above it:
#   # added: YYYY-MM-DD       when it was first allowlisted
#   # reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD    when someone last re-checked the reason
#   # review-by: YYYY-MM-DD   at most 180 days after `reviewed`
# The scan fails once a review-by date has passed, so an entry cannot outlive
# its reason unnoticed; tools/tests/test_trivyignore.py enforces the format.
# To keep an entry, re-check the reason, then move `reviewed` and `review-by`.

# pip's vendored setuptools. Trivy reads src/pip/_vendor/vendor.txt and reports
# the packages pip bundles for its own use as if they were installed. pip 26.3
# — the newest release, which the Dockerfile installs — still vendors
# setuptools 70.3.0 (pkg_resources). Nothing in this application imports it:
# pip uses pkg_resources for its own metadata handling, at build time, on input
# pip itself fetched over TLS — and since 1.8.0 every wheel it installs is
# checked against app/requirements.lock's hashes. Upgrading pip changes nothing
# until pypa re-vendors. (pip 26.3 re-vendored msgpack and urllib3, so their
# entries are gone.)
# Since the runtime/dev split (audit O-2) the image built from this tree has no
# pip at all — app/Dockerfile's `runtime` stage uninstalls it — so the scans of
# a fresh build (security-scan.yml `image`, publish.yml's pre-push scans) no
# longer meet this finding. It stays for security-scan.yml's `published` job,
# which scans the `:1` and `latest` already on GHCR: remove this entry once
# both have been rebuilt from a commit with the pip-less runtime stage.
# added: 2026-09-09
# reviewed: 2026-11-15
# review-by: 2027-02-15
CVE-2025-47273
